<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="types-fixbug">
  <title>Fix the Bug</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Runestone cannot count how many lines a student changed: hidden tests
      run as Python <em>after</em> the student's code and cannot see its
      source. The line budget is therefore either honour-based, enforced by
      locking code, or replaced by a two-step <q>find it, then fix it</q>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-fix-find">
    <title>Step 1: find the buggy line (clickable area)</title>
    <p>
      The student clicks the line they think is wrong. This grades
      <em>locating</em> the bug separately from repairing it. CodeRunner
      cannot do this.
    </p>
    <exercise label="fix-areas-seed">
      <statement>
        <p>For <c>[5, 3, 8, 9]</c>, this <c>find_min</c> returns <c>0</c>.
        Click the line that causes the bug.</p>
      </statement>
      <areas language="python">
        <cline><area correct="no">def find_min(numbers):</area></cline>
        <cline>    <area correct="yes">best = 0</area></cline>
        <cline>    <area correct="no">for i in range(1, len(numbers)):</area></cline>
        <cline>        <area correct="no">if numbers[i] &lt; best:</area></cline>
        <cline>            <area correct="no">best = numbers[i]</area></cline>
        <cline>    <area correct="no">return best</area></cline>
      </areas>
      <feedback>
        <p>Every number in the list is above 0, so nothing ever beats the
        starting value. The seed must be a real element, <c>numbers[0]</c>.</p>
      </feedback>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-fix-activecode">
    <title>Step 2: fix it in ActiveCode (tests enforced, budget on trust)</title>
    <exercise label="fix-ac-seed">
      <statement>
        <p>Fix the bug by changing <alert>one line only</alert>. Press
        <em>Run</em> to run the tests. Use <em>Reset</em> to start again.</p>
      </statement>
      <program language="python" interactive="activecode" label="ac-fix-seed">
        <code><![CDATA[
def find_min(numbers):
    best = 0
    for i in range(1, len(numbers)):
        if numbers[i] < best:
            best = numbers[i]
    return best

print(find_min([5, 3, 8, 9]))
]]></code>
        <tests><![CDATA[
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class Tests(TestCaseGui):
    def test_examples(self):
        self.assertEqual(find_min([5, 3, 8, 9]), 3, "find_min([5, 3, 8, 9])")
        self.assertEqual(find_min([7]), 7, "a single element")
        self.assertEqual(find_min([-3, -8, -1]), -8, "negative numbers")

Tests().main()
]]></tests>
      </program>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-fix-locked">
    <title>Budget enforced by locking</title>
    <p>
      Only the lines around the bug are editable, and everything else is a
      locked preamble or postamble. This enforces the budget but shows where
      the bug is, so use it after the clickable-area step, or when the skill
      is the <em>repair</em> rather than the search.
    </p>
    <exercise label="fix-locked-overwrite">
      <title>Overwriting every time</title>
      <statement>
        <p>This <c>find_min</c> always returns the last element. Fix the loop body.</p>
      </statement>
      <program language="python" interactive="activecode" label="ac-fix-locked">
        <preamble visible="yes"><![CDATA[
def find_min(numbers):
    best = numbers[0]
    for i in range(1, len(numbers)):
]]></preamble>
        <code><![CDATA[
        best = numbers[i]
]]></code>
        <postamble visible="yes"><![CDATA[
    return best
]]></postamble>
        <tests><![CDATA[
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class Tests(TestCaseGui):
    def test_examples(self):
        self.assertEqual(find_min([5, 3, 8, 9]), 3, "find_min([5, 3, 8, 9])")
        self.assertEqual(find_min([5, 3, 8, 1]), 1, "find_min([5, 3, 8, 1])")

Tests().main()
]]></tests>
      </program>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-fix-choose">
    <title>Choose the repair (Parsons with one paired block)</title>
    <p>
      A fully recognition-based version: the program is given in order, and
      at the buggy line the student chooses between the original and
      candidate repairs.
    </p>
    <exercise label="fix-parsons-offbyone" language="python" indentation="show">
      <statement>
        <p>This <c>find_min</c> never checks the last element. Arrange the
        lines and choose the correct loop header.</p>
      </statement>
      <blocks>
        <block><cline>def find_min(numbers):</cline></block>
        <block><cline>    best = numbers[0]</cline></block>
        <block>
          <choice correct="yes"><cline>    for i in range(1, len(numbers)):</cline></choice>
          <choice><cline>    for i in range(1, len(numbers) - 1):</cline></choice>
          <choice><cline>    for i in range(1, len(numbers) + 1):</cline></choice>
        </block>
        <block><cline>        if numbers[i] &lt; best:</cline></block>
        <block><cline>            best = numbers[i]</cline></block>
        <block><cline>    return best</cline></block>
      </blocks>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
</section>
